WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
In chapter 1, the microtubules placed the band, and the cut fell between the two

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.320
sets of chromosomes.

3
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.420
But the cut splits everything else too.

4
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.940
Does the cell count its organelles out, or is chance good enough?

5
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.130
Start with the simplest model: no control at all.

6
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:26.830
Each copy of an organelle goes to either daughter by a coin toss, on its own.

7
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:37.673
With a hundred copies, the count in one daughter averages fifty, give or take about

8
00:00:37.673 --> 00:00:41.093
five. Most daughters get forty to sixty.

9
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:47.773
The chance that either daughter gets none is about 1 .6 times 10 to the minus 30.

10
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:50.913
In practice, that never happens.

11
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:58.557
Now shrink the stock to just two copies, tossed the same way.

12
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:02.137
How often does one daughter end up with none?

13
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:14.230
Half the time. Both copies can land left, or both right; each happens one time in

14
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:19.710
four. Either daughter can be the empty one, so together they make one in two.

15
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.730
The chance of an empty daughter is two times one half to the power n.

16
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.750
Each added copy halves the risk.

17
00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.570
At ten copies it is about one in five hundred.

18
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.870
At twenty-one copies it drops below one in a million.

19
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.353
So chance is safe only for many independent units.

20
00:01:44.493 --> 00:01:49.333
But some organelles are one connected structure, like a mammalian cell's Golgi

21
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.173
ribbon, or a tubular mitochondrial network.

22
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:55.513
To the coin, each is a single copy.

23
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:03.887
Such a structure must be broken up first, or moved actively.

24
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:11.147
In PtK1 cells, a rat-kangaroo kidney line, the Golgi ribbon comes apart during

25
00:02:11.147 --> 00:02:15.767
mitosis. Its membranes cluster around the spindle poles.

26
00:02:17.107 --> 00:02:19.007
After division, the ribbon reforms.

27
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.770
Mitochondria in HeLa cells, a human cell line, broke into fragments early in

28
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:32.750
mitosis. Without Drp1, a protein that drives mitochondrial fission, they stayed

29
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:37.430
tubular. That is one cell line, so treat it as a strong lead.

30
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.900
Some things are not left to chance.

31
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.620
The two centrosomes sit at the spindle poles, and the spindle carries one to each

32
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.780
pole. So microtubules return: they place the cut, and carry part of the inheritance.

33
00:02:55.983 --> 00:03:01.443
One caution. Moving an organelle is not the same as changing its share.

34
00:03:02.743 --> 00:03:04.283
Each claim needs its own test.

35
00:03:09.017 --> 00:03:11.237
So the cell need not count every unit.

36
00:03:12.677 --> 00:03:16.337
Chance can share many small units, often with some control alongside.

37
00:03:17.817 --> 00:03:22.197
Chance is safe only for many units; one connected unit needs control.
